WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.817
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Algorithm Analysis
Basics as a question about inputs, decisions,

2
00:00:07.817 --> 00:00:09.609
and observable results.

3
00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:15.946
NARRATOR: Algorithm analysis connects a
precise problem specification to a correctness

4
00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:18.250
argument and resource bounds.

5
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:25.781
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A sorting
algorithm must guarantee that the output array

6
00:00:25.781 --> 00:00:29.546
contains all input elements in non-decreasing
order.

7
00:00:29.546 --> 00:00:35.343
NARRATOR: On screen, display a split screen
showing a problem specification on the left

8
00:00:35.343 --> 00:00:38.656
and a resource bound graph on the right.

9
00:00:38.656 --> 00:00:44.622
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

10
00:00:44.622 --> 00:00:45.999
change the conclusion.

11
00:00:45.999 --> 00:00:51.906
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:58.748
NARRATOR: Now define Asymptotic Notation
precisely enough that another learner could

13
00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:00.585
test the definition.

14
00:01:00.585 --> 00:01:07.272
NARRATOR: Asymptotic notation describes how
resource use grows with input size.

15
00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:14.158
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Big-O
notation indicates that a search algorithm's

16
00:01:14.158 --> 00:01:19.897
time complexity increases proportionally to
the number of items searched.

17
00:01:19.897 --> 00:01:27.774
NARRATOR: On screen, show a line graph where
the x-axis represents input size and the

18
00:01:27.774 --> 00:01:31.975
y-axis represents resource usage, highlighting
a linear trend.

19
00:01:31.975 --> 00:01:38.713
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

20
00:01:38.713 --> 00:01:40.959
evidence would distinguish them.

21
00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:47.428
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

22
00:01:47.428 --> 00:01:54.451
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

23
00:01:54.451 --> 00:01:56.366
throughout the analysis.

24
00:01:56.474 --> 00:02:02.768
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Loop Invariants operates in a

25
00:02:02.768 --> 00:02:03.736
