26
00:02:03.736 --> 00:02:10.361
NARRATOR: A loop invariant supports a proof by
initialization, maintenance, and termination.

27
00:02:10.361 --> 00:02:15.911
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: In a
for-loop that sums an array, the invariant

28
00:02:15.911 --> 00:02:21.064
states that the running total equals the sum
of the first i elements.

29
00:02:21.064 --> 00:02:28.122
NARRATOR: On screen, animate a loop counter
and a running total, updating them

30
00:02:28.122 --> 00:02:31.923
step-by-step to demonstrate the invariant
holding true.

31
00:02:31.923 --> 00:02:38.814
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

32
00:02:38.814 --> 00:02:44.345
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

33
00:02:44.345 --> 00:02:51.883
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

34
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:53.939
throughout the analysis.

35
00:02:54.050 --> 00:03:00.221
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Divide-and-Conquer and make the decision

36
00:03:00.221 --> 00:03:01.250
criteria explicit.

37
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:07.565
NARRATOR: Divide-and-conquer algorithms split
a problem, solve subproblems, and combine

38
00:03:07.565 --> 00:03:08.828
